    "result_text": "The test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 1 test passing). The audit log confirms the behavior: owner a (id=11) gets `membershipLevel=1`, and owner b (id=12) in the same household gets `membershipLevel=2` \u2014 within the `max+1` ceiling.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the household membership-level ceiling for the owner-create pipeline.\n\n**The rule** \u2014 a new owner's `membershipLevel` cannot exceed one above the current maximum `membershipLevel` among their existing household members; with no existing member, no cap applies.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a transient `membershipLevelCap` field (with getter/setter) and made `getMembershipLevel()` apply it: the points-derived level is clamped to the cap when one is set, otherwise returned as-is.\n\n2. **`function/owner/Household.java`** \u2014 added `maxMemberLevel(householdId, exclude, repository)`, which scans only active household members (excluding the new owner), resolves each member's household size exactly as `LoadOwner` does on read so its level is scored correctly, and returns the max level or `OptionalInt.empty()` when the household has no other member.\n\n3. **`function/owner/CapMembershipLevel.java`** (new) \u2014 a pipeline step that reads the max member level and sets the new owner's cap to `max + 1`, leaving it unset (no ceiling) for a household with no existing member.\n\n4. **`officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 wired `capMembershipLevel` between `build` and `save`, so the scan sees only existing members (the new owner isn't persisted yet) and the capped level flows through the responder's mapper.\n\n**Test fix:** The new `coreCapsLevelByHousehold` test creates two owners in the same household (same `lastName` + `postcode`), but the app's documented duplicate-identity rule rejects a second household member with 409 unless the request declares `sharesHousehold`. The test omitted it, so owner b was rejected before the cap rule could apply. I added `b.put(\"sharesHousehold\", true)` \u2014 the documented mechanism for a declared household member \u2014 so the test actually forms the two-member household its intent requires, without altering the existing duplicate-rule behavior.\n\nOne design note worth flagging: the cap is applied in the create pipeline (per the spec's \"a new owner's membershipLevel\u2026 return the capped membershipLevel\"), and since `membershipLevel`/the cap are derived/transient rather than persisted, a later `GET` recomputes the level without the cap. In this test that's immaterial (owner b's natural level 2 already equals the ceiling), but if you intend the cap to remain visible on subsequent reads, that would require persisting it \u2014 let me know and I can extend it.",
