    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots cleanly into the established conventions of this codebase:\n\n- **`Household.maxMemberLevel`** is the right home. It sits directly beside the sibling household lookups `hasMember` and `size`, shares their exact signature shape `(householdId, exclude, ownerRepository)`, the same `ActiveOwners`-only scan, the same null/blank guard, and the same doc style cross-referencing the other members. A maintainer would expect a new household-scoped query to land here, and it does.\n\n- **`CapMembershipLevel`** is a dedicated single-`service`-method step function, matching the one-function-per-step pipeline convention, and it's wired between `build` and `save` with `govern: [ transaction ]` like its neighbors \u2014 the comment even explains the ordering rationale, consistent with the existing YAML comments. The \"mutate the owner in place\" approach matches the `@Val` idiom the spec describes.\n\n- **The `Owner` model changes** follow the existing pattern precisely: `membershipLevelCap` is another pipeline-populated `@Transient` field (like `householdSize`, `namesakeCount`, `bulkSignupWarning`) feeding a derived getter, and the cap is applied inside `getMembershipLevel()` \u2014 the natural place, since that getter is what the mapper carries to the DTO. The early-return chain was refactored to an if/else-assign-then-cap form, which is the minimal clean way to layer the ceiling on after derivation. Javadoc on both the field and the getter was updated to document the new behavior.\n\nLogic is in the right unit, nothing is duplicated beyond the household-scan shape already idiomatic to `Household`, and no class is becoming a catch-all. (Noted but out of scope per the review instructions: `maxMemberLevel` recomputes `size(...)` per member inside the loop \u2014 an efficiency/correctness matter, not a structural one.)",
